---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Combat Damage Formula"
  active="combat-damage"
  pageMark="⚔️"
  pageStats={[
    { label: 'reference', value: 'transcribed' },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Old World resolves a combat by comparing each side's effective strength,
    then dealing damage proportional to the ratio. The expressions below are
    the standard formulation used by the legacy reference spreadsheet, with
    every constant cross-checked against the game's <code>globalsInt.xml</code>.
    Where modifiers compound, the order matters — they apply multiplicatively
    in the order listed.
  </p>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">The core idea</h2>
    <p>
      Each unit has a base <strong>Strength</strong> (e.g., Hoplite 5,
      Horseman 5, Catapult 4) and <strong>HP</strong> (typically 20). The
      attacker and defender each compute an <em>effective strength</em> for
      this specific clash, and damage is dealt in proportion to that ratio.
      Both sides take damage; the attacker just takes less when winning the
      ratio.
    </p>

    <div class="cd-formula">
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">DamageToDefender = HP_attacker × ( StrAtt / StrDef ) / 2</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">DamageToAttacker = HP_defender × ( StrDef / StrAtt ) / 2</span>
      </div>
      <p class="cd-note">
        With <code>StrAtt</code> and <code>StrDef</code> the effective strengths
        after modifiers, and <code>HP</code> the unit's max HP.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">Effective strength</h2>
    <p>
      Effective strength stacks every modifier that applies to <em>this</em>
      attack — terrain, fortification, promotions, anti-trait bonuses,
      flanking, and remaining HP penalty.
    </p>

    <div class="cd-formula">
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">
          StrEff = BaseStr × (1 + Σ Modifiers) × (HP_current / HP_max)
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="cd-h3">Common modifiers</h3>
    <ul class="cd-list">
      <li><strong>Promotions:</strong> Combat I/II/III give +5 / +10 / +15% strength. Strike I/II/III give +10/+15/+20% attack only. Guard I/II/III give +10/+15/+20% defense only.</li>
      <li><strong>Anti-trait:</strong> Spearman has <em>Anti-Mounted II</em> = +50% strength vs Mounted in melee. Axeman has <em>Anti-Polearm</em> = +25% strength vs Polearm.</li>
      <li><strong>Hill:</strong> +25% defense when fighting from a Hill (unless attacker has <em>Highlander</em>).</li>
      <li><strong>Vegetation:</strong> +25% defense in Trees or Jungle (negated by <em>Ranger</em>).</li>
      <li><strong>Fortify:</strong> +25% defense when fortified for 1+ turn, capped at +50%.</li>
      <li><strong>Flanking:</strong> +10% attack per additional friendly unit adjacent to the defender (caps at 3 flankers = +30%).</li>
      <li><strong>Damaged units:</strong> a unit at half HP fights at half its current strength — the (HP_current / HP_max) factor at the end of the formula.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">Worked example: Hoplite attacks Horseman</h2>
    <p>
      Both sides at full HP. Hoplite is Polearm with <em>Anti-Mounted II</em>
      = +50% vs Mounted. Horseman is on open terrain with no other modifiers.
    </p>
    <div class="cd-table">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hoplite base strength</td><td>50 (display: 5)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>+ Anti-Mounted II</td><td>×1.50</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hoplite effective strength</td><td><strong>75</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Horseman base strength</td><td>50 (display: 5)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>(no defensive modifiers)</td><td>×1.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Horseman effective strength</td><td><strong>50</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="cd-formula">
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">DamageToHorseman = 20 × (75 / 50) / 2 = <strong>15 HP</strong></span>
      </div>
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">DamageToHoplite  = 20 × (50 / 75) / 2 ≈ <strong>6.7 HP</strong></span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="cd-note">
      So the Horseman drops to 5 HP (close to rout) while the Hoplite ends
      around 13 HP. A second clash kills the Horseman before he can land
      meaningful damage — which is why "spear beats horse" is such a hard rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">Worked example: Archer (ranged) at full range</h2>
    <p>
      Ranged units fight without taking return damage, but their <em>effective
      strength</em> drops at maximum range. The formula is the same; only the
      attacker side fires.
    </p>
    <div class="cd-table">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Archer base strength</td><td>40</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Max range fire (no melee return)</td><td>×1.00</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Target: Spearman strength</td><td>50</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="cd-formula">
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">DamageToSpearman = 20 × (40 / 50) / 2 = <strong>8 HP</strong></span>
      </div>
      <div class="cd-line">
        <span class="cd-eq">DamageToArcher = <strong>0</strong> (out of melee range)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">Attack patterns (Pierce, Cleave, Splash, Circle)</h2>
    <p>
      Some units carry an <em>attack pattern</em> on top of the main hit. These
      are additional small attacks that resolve against adjacent / linear /
      surrounding tiles. They use the same damage formula but with a reduced
      effective strength, and the attacker doesn't take return damage from
      those secondary hits.
    </p>
    <ul class="cd-list">
      <li><strong>Pierce</strong> — Spearman / Pikeman line. Hits the primary target plus the tile behind it.</li>
      <li><strong>Cleave</strong> — Axeman / Maceman line. Hits the primary plus the two flanking tiles.</li>
      <li><strong>Splash</strong> — Catapult / Trebuchet. Hits a 7-tile cluster.</li>
      <li><strong>Circle</strong> — Macedonian Companion / certain elephants. Hits all 6 adjacent tiles.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Per the game data, the secondary-hit strength is the unit's base
      multiplied by a small percentage (e.g. <em>Pierce I</em> = +25% Pierce
      damage on top of a flat +1 to the pattern's value).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">Rout and routed units</h2>
    <p>
      A unit at 0 HP doesn't disappear — it <strong>routs</strong>, retreats
      one tile, and becomes unable to attack until rallied. The standard
      damage formula caps at HP remaining; the killing blow only does enough
      damage to bring it to 0.
    </p>
    <ul class="cd-list">
      <li>Units that can't retreat (surrounded, water, ZoC) are destroyed instead.</li>
      <li>A routed unit at 0 HP heals at the active-heal rate next turn (5 HP/turn) once safe.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">Constants pulled from XML</h2>
    <p class="cd-note">
      These come straight from <code>globalsInt.xml</code> and apply to combat
      math globally. Some are display-friendly multipliers; most are integers.
    </p>
    <div class="cd-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Constant</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>STRENGTH_MULTIPLIER</code></td><td>10</td><td>Game-internal strength is 10× the display value.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ACTIVE_HEAL</code></td><td>5</td><td>HP healed per turn when not moving / attacking.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>IDLE_HEAL</code></td><td>1</td><td>HP healed per turn after moving but not attacking.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>UNIT_HEAL_COST</code></td><td>1</td><td>Cost per HP healed (in Training or Civics, depending).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>COUNTER_CITY_DAMAGE</code></td><td>1</td><td>Damage delivered to a city per attack against its garrison.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>COUNTER_ROUT_DAMAGE</code></td><td>1</td><td>Damage applied to a routed unit if attacked again.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CITY_STRENGTH_BASE</code></td><td>80</td><td>Base garrison strength for an undamaged city.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CITY_STRENGTH_PER</code></td><td>5</td><td>Bonus strength per city level.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CITY_HEAL_PERCENT_PER_TURN</code></td><td>5%</td><td>City HP healed per turn (peacetime).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CITY_CAPTURE_DAMAGE_PERCENT</code></td><td>50%</td><td>City damage taken on capture.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cd-section">
    <h2 class="cd-h">What to read next</h2>
    <ul class="cd-list">
      <li><a href={`${base}unit-damage`}>Unit Damage &amp; Counters</a> — full stat cards for every combat unit, including each unit's anti-trait modifiers.</li>
      <li><a href={`${base}unit-counters`}>Unit Counters at-a-glance</a> — the rock-paper-scissors matrix that summarises this page in one grid.</li>
      <li><a href={`${base}promotions`}>Promotions</a> — every numeric modifier you can stack onto effective strength.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    .lede {
      color: var(--text-dim);
      max-width: 72ch;
      margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
    }
    .lede code {
      background: var(--bg-elev);
      padding: 0.05rem 0.35rem;
      border-radius: 3px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .cd-section {
      margin: 1.6rem 0;
      max-width: 78ch;
    }
    .cd-h {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
      margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 25%, var(--border));
      padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .cd-h3 {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      margin: 1rem 0 0.35rem;
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .cd-section p {
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 0.86rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    }
    .cd-section p em { color: var(--accent); font-style: normal; }
    .cd-section p strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
    .cd-section code {
      background: var(--bg-elev);
      padding: 0.05rem 0.35rem;
      border-radius: 3px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      font-size: 0.85em;
    }
    .cd-list {
      margin: 0.3rem 0 0.8rem 0;
      padding-left: 1.1rem;
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
    }
    .cd-list li { margin: 0.2rem 0; }
    .cd-list em { color: var(--accent); font-style: normal; }
    .cd-formula {
      background: var(--bg-elev);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
      margin: 0.6rem 0;
    }
    .cd-line {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--text);
      padding: 0.2rem 0;
    }
    .cd-eq strong { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
    .cd-note {
      font-size: 0.78rem !important;
      color: var(--text-dim) !important;
      font-style: italic;
      margin-top: 0.4rem !important;
    }
    .cd-table {
      margin: 0.5rem 0 0.9rem;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }
    .cd-table table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }
    .cd-table th, .cd-table td {
      text-align: left;
      padding: 0.32rem 0.55rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .cd-table th {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.65rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      font-weight: 500;
      background: var(--bg-elev);
    }
    .cd-table td:last-child {
      color: var(--text-dim);
    }
    .cd-table strong { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
    .cd-section a { color: var(--accent-2); }
  </style>
</Base>
